  "summary": "Minimalist flower arrangement for tea ceremony emphasizing naturalness and impermanence",
  "description": "Characterized by simple, unarranged flowers in rustic containers; valued for their seasonal appropriateness and humble appearance, rejecting elaborate floristry in favor of nature's inherent beauty.",
  "culturalContext": "The history of chabana follows hand-in-hand with the history of chadō, and within that historical milieu, chabana emerged in tandem with the rise of wabi-cha around the Momoyama period. Sen no Rikyū is considered the most influential person in the development of wabi-cha, and is also credited as the originator of the accompanying nageire mode of flower arrangement, which is characterized by freedom and spontaneity in expressing the natural beauty of the material. Among the statements attributed to him, the first one in the set of seven known as \"Rikyū's Seven Precepts\" (Rikyū shichisoku) concerns chabana.  It goes, \"The flowers [Chabana] should be such as they are in the moor.\" In the history of ikebana, the nageire style was added to the more stylized rikka arrangement around the end of the 1600s, as influenced by chabana. The term \"chabana\" was first used in 1765 during the Meiwa period. It is believed to first appeared in The Tale of Genji.",
